    *   For all subsequent words, `word.charAt(0).toUpperCase() + word.slice(1)` takes the first character, makes it uppercase, and concatenates it with the rest of the word. For example, `'function'` becomes `'Function'`.
3.  **`.join('')`**: This joins the elements of the transformed array back into a single string with no separator between them.

### Comparison

| Method | Pros | Cons |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Regex** | - Very concise and efficient. <br> - Considered the standard approach for this kind of string manipulation. | - Regex syntax can be less intuitive for beginners. |
| **split/map/join** | - Highly readable and easy to understand the logic step-by-step. <br> - Good demonstration of functional programming. | - Slightly more verbose. <br> - Can be marginally less performant due to the creation of an intermediate array. |

For most use cases, either function will work perfectly. The **Regex** version is generally preferred for its conciseness and performance.

